WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.169 --> 00:00:03.800
- I had no idea how I was
gonna return this lost iPhone.

00:00:03.800 --> 00:00:06.560
But by God I was determined
to figure it out.

00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:09.643
(lighthearted music)

00:00:11.667 --> 00:00:12.869
I went out to Long Island this weekend

00:00:12.869 --> 00:00:15.571
with my girlfriend and Sunday
evening we jumped on a train

00:00:15.571 --> 00:00:17.461
to head back into New York City.

00:00:17.461 --> 00:00:20.519
Right away we notice an
iPhone wedged between

00:00:20.519 --> 00:00:22.669
the seat and the wall.

00:00:22.669 --> 00:00:25.029
Now we could have given
the phone to a conductor,

00:00:25.029 --> 00:00:27.240
but we had more than two hours to kill,

00:00:27.240 --> 00:00:29.657
and I stared thinking, WWEDD.

00:00:30.851 --> 00:00:33.261
What would Ellen DeGeneres do.

00:00:33.261 --> 00:00:34.821
My girlfriend, Stephanie, starts googling

00:00:34.821 --> 00:00:37.080
what to do if you find a lost iPhone.

00:00:37.080 --> 00:00:39.701
But if the phone is locked,
and the owner never filled out

00:00:39.701 --> 00:00:43.101
the emergency info, then
there's very few options

00:00:43.101 --> 00:00:44.434
outside of Siri.

00:00:49.899 --> 00:00:52.229
We asked Siri anything we can think of.

00:00:52.229 --> 00:00:53.676
Siri, call home.

00:00:53.676 --> 00:00:57.843
Siri, call Mom, Dad, Michael,
Emily, Arnold, anything,

00:00:59.069 --> 00:01:01.902
but Siri has nothing that matches.

00:01:08.303 --> 00:01:11.792
Stephanie then asks Siri to
dial the most recent call.

00:01:11.792 --> 00:01:15.292
So, Siri FaceTime Audio calls Ryan's cell.

00:01:17.692 --> 00:01:21.873
So we ask Siri to text Ryan's
cell and dictate the message

00:01:21.873 --> 00:01:25.932
word for word 'cause remember,
we can't unlock the phone.

00:01:25.932 --> 00:01:28.913
We ask Ryan's cell to call
the number of the lost phone

00:01:28.913 --> 00:01:30.580
so we can swap info.

00:01:36.153 --> 00:01:39.223
I then start to think about the iCloud.

00:01:39.223 --> 00:01:42.572
If the phone owner has his
photos connected to the cloud,

00:01:42.572 --> 00:01:45.393
then he'll see any photo or video I take.

00:01:45.393 --> 00:01:49.560
I have access to the camera,
so, I send a message.

00:01:52.975 --> 00:01:54.353
But, it doesn't matter.

00:01:54.353 --> 00:01:58.436
A few minutes later, Ryan's
cell calls the phone.

00:02:04.022 --> 00:02:07.295
Ryan, turns out, is the best
friend of the phone owner,

00:02:07.295 --> 00:02:10.233
who's name is Kirk and lives
in the town of Bayshore,

00:02:10.233 --> 00:02:13.201
which is halfway between
where we left and where

00:02:13.201 --> 00:02:15.632
Stephanie and I live in New York City.

00:02:15.632 --> 00:02:18.844
I text Ryan so he has my number.

00:02:18.844 --> 00:02:22.703
Please forward my info
along to Kirk, or Kurt.

00:02:22.703 --> 00:02:25.369
It was a loud train, I
couldn't really hear the name.

00:02:25.369 --> 00:02:27.827
Ryan passes the info to Kirk.

00:02:27.827 --> 00:02:31.155
About 40 minutes into our train
ride, I get a text message

00:02:31.155 --> 00:02:32.238
from a Chris.

00:02:35.107 --> 00:02:38.616
I tell her our next stop is
Bayshore, but I'm not able

00:02:38.616 --> 00:02:40.366
to get off the train.

00:02:44.826 --> 00:02:47.415
Now, why couldn't I get off the train?

00:02:47.415 --> 00:02:50.613
For one, you could wait
up to an hour for a train

00:02:50.613 --> 00:02:52.215
on the LIRR.

00:02:52.215 --> 00:02:54.135
Two, it was raining and it was cold.

00:02:54.135 --> 00:02:55.597
I was trying to do a nice deed.

00:02:55.597 --> 00:02:57.575
I wasn't trying to get pneumonia.

00:02:57.575 --> 00:03:00.645
Three, we had our cat with us,
who was already hating life

00:03:00.645 --> 00:03:02.946
because he hates traveling.

00:03:02.946 --> 00:03:05.362
We'll be there in the next two minutes.

00:03:05.362 --> 00:03:08.279
She texts, I live two minutes away.

00:03:15.986 --> 00:03:20.186
The train finally pulls
up, I poke my head out,

00:03:20.186 --> 00:03:22.103
and I don't see anyone.

00:03:33.007 --> 00:03:34.757
Just as the train doors close,

00:03:34.757 --> 00:03:38.458
I get a text telling me
to put the phone outside.

00:03:38.458 --> 00:03:40.917
'Cause they're stuck on the
other side of the tracks.

00:03:40.917 --> 00:03:43.750
But, at this point, it's too late.

00:04:04.951 --> 00:04:07.530
I sent a photo so they would know exactly

00:04:07.530 --> 00:04:09.218
where the phone is.

00:04:09.218 --> 00:04:12.647
Not two minutes later, I get confirmation

00:04:12.647 --> 00:04:15.397
that they've picked up the phone.

00:04:16.252 --> 00:04:20.252
The whole ordeal took exactly 58 minutes.

00:04:20.252 --> 00:04:22.451
So, Kirk got his phone back.

00:04:22.451 --> 00:04:24.391
Stephanie and I did a good deed.

00:04:24.391 --> 00:04:26.151
Buzzfeed got a video.

00:04:26.151 --> 00:04:27.551
Everyone was happy.

00:04:27.551 --> 00:04:30.634
Except our cat, who was still pissed.

00:04:31.811 --> 00:04:34.394
(gentle music)

